Two organisations, and the three tenants that serve them
Above the deployment, two kinds of thing that are not inside it. Applications
ask the store: the clinic's own application over FHIR, and a host that embeds
the store in its own JVM, each carrying a token from that tenant's own issuer.
Participants are offered work: a bench instrument behind a router and a
supplier's own system elsewhere, each holding an enrolment and taking work it
asked for. Inside one node are five tenants. Three supply: a zone holding which
brokers authenticate and which identifiers prove, a face root holding a FHIR
version's structures, searches, value sets and codes, and a managing tenant
holding what this deployment was told to serve. Two are served: the clinic and
the laboratory, each with its own issuer, keys and people. What the zone and
the face root hold moves down one way, only where the tenant below declared it,
read-only where it lands and stamped with where it came from, and never
carrying a person. Beneath, five databases, one per tenant. Nothing reads
another tenant's records, for anybody, the operator included.
